# Cloud Build source-upload allowlist for the compliance-scanner
# signature-refresh pipeline (services/compliance-scanner/cloudbuild.signature-refresh.yaml).
#
# Root cause this fixes: this is a Flutter monorepo. Every
# `gcloud builds submit` from the repository root previously uploaded
# the ENTIRE working tree — ~1 GiB, dominated by committed native
# CocoaPods/Gradle SDK binaries under ios/, ios_backup/, macos/,
# windows/, linux/ (GoogleMaps, Google-Mobile-Ads-SDK,
# RecaptchaEnterpriseSDK, GoogleAppMeasurement frameworks — ~893 MiB /
# 87% of the total, none of it gitignored) plus marketing/demo assets
# (assets/, *.gif, *.mp4) — none of which this pipeline ever reads.
#
# Every step in cloudbuild.signature-refresh.yaml, every services/compliance-scanner/ci/*.sh
# and ci/*.js file, and services/compliance-scanner/Dockerfile were traced directly (grep
# across the full pipeline for any path reference) and confirmed to
# read ONLY files under services/compliance-scanner/ — nothing at the
# repository root, nothing under lib/, functions/, ios/, android/,
# macos/, windows/, linux/, assets/, docs/, or any other top-level
# directory. services/compliance-scanner/ci/verify-source.sh in
# particular never reads .git — it only runs `npm ci`, the local test
# suite, and node/sh syntax checks, all confined to
# services/compliance-scanner/ (confirmed by reading its own source).
#
# This is therefore a narrow ALLOWLIST, not a denylist of individually
# observed large directories — a denylist would need to be updated
# every time someone adds a new large asset anywhere else in this
# monorepo; an allowlist scoped to the one subtree this pipeline
# actually touches cannot regress that way.
#
# Uses the standard, documented gitignore technique for allowlisting one
# nested directory (see `git help gitignore`, NOTES section: a parent
# directory excluded by a wildcard must be re-included explicitly
# before its own contents can be re-included — this is documented,
# well-known git/gcloudignore behavior, not an undocumented ordering
# dependency). Verified empirically after writing this file via
# `gcloud meta list-files-for-upload` (local-only, no network/project
# access) to confirm the actual resulting file set, not merely assumed
# from reading this pattern.

# 1. Ignore everything at the repository root by default.
/*

# 2. Re-include the one top-level directory this pipeline needs...
!/services

# 3. ...but ignore everything one level inside it again (services/ has
#    exactly one child today — compliance-scanner — confirmed via `ls
#    services/` — this line is what makes the allowlist safe against a
#    future unrelated services/<other-service>/ directory being
#    silently swept in).
/services/*

# 4. ...then re-include the exact one subtree the pipeline reads,
#    recursively (gitignore's `!dir` after a parent `!` re-inclusion
#    correctly re-includes the directory AND everything inside it).
!/services/compliance-scanner

# .git is never needed by this pipeline (verify-source.sh confirmed to
# never touch it) and is excluded explicitly rather than relied upon as
# an implicit default.
/.git

# Local node_modules (present on this machine from local testing, not
# from any pipeline requirement): install-dependencies.sh installs its
# own fresh copy from package-lock.json inside Cloud Build every run
# (`npm ci`, which always deletes and reinstalls regardless of what
# already exists) — uploading a local copy would be redundant, and
# node_modules is explicitly the kind of local build output this
# allowlist must not sweep in just because it happens to sit inside the
# one directory being re-included above.
/services/compliance-scanner/node_modules/
